# <%= current_page.data.title %>

## Introduction

This guide sets out the resources that have been deployed & configured to allow use of AWS Transform from our core-shared-services account.

It is not meant to be a comprehensive guide on the use of Transform, but rather what we have set up in that account, the reasons why and any issues to be aware of.

## How AWS Transform was Deployed

As of writing, Transform is a relatively new service offered by AWS and as such there is little to no support for the product either in terraform, nor in the AWS CLI.

The Transform service was deployed in core-shared-services via the console with the following features enabled:

- Deployed into region eu-west-2

- A central S3 bucket, bucket policy plus a kms key & policy to hold configurations was added, the terraform for which is in ../../terraform/environments/core-shared-services/transform.tf

- Link to Identity Centre in MOJ Master for SSO access to Transform. This will be the means of access most typically used.

- Access to Transform via IAM Role to allow use of the service via the AdministratorAccess role should SSO be unavailable.

## End User Access to Transform

AWS provides URLs to access the Transform application. This service is hosted externally and not in a VPC. This URL can be found in the settings page of the console (https://eu-west-2.console.aws.amazon.com/transform/serviceManagement?region=eu-west-2)

SSO users or groups are added and there is a direct lookup to Identity Centre when a user or group name is entered.

## Transform Workspaces, Jobs and Connectors.

Whilst these are listed in the AWS Console, they can only be added from Transform as part of its workflow.

Similarly, associating users with workspaces and their permissions in a workspace is also undertaken via the Transform app.

## AWS CodeConnections

For certain migration types, Transform makes use of AWS CodeConnections to support access to github repositories. When created, the CodeConnection is then linked to a github app via a user with github org admin permissions.

CodeConnection itself does not hold any permissions to github.

Two CodeConnections have been created to date. These are also in the above-mentioned terraform file (transform.tf)

The CodeConnection "mp-aws-transform-github" should be the default one used.

## Assumable IAM Role MemberSharedServicesTransform

Transform has its own CLI (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/transform/latest/userguide/custom-get-started.html) that can be used from the user's own device to access Transform features. 

To support this use, an additional role (MemberSharedServicesTransform) was added via the above terraform to allow the user to access these permissions.

